meta {
  name: List group streams
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{groupService}}/streams?groupKey={{groupKeyQuery}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  groupKey: {{groupKeyQuery}}
}

script:pre-request {
  bru.setVar("groupKeyQuery", encodeURIComponent(bru.getEnvVar("groupKey") || ""));
}

tests {
  test("answers this group's prefix and the streams under it", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.prefix).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("groupPrefix"));
    expect(res.body.streams).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  # List group streams

  Answers which streams the relay carries under this group key's prefix, one row per stream: the name inside the group, whether it is carrying anything yet, its tracks, and the video format.

  The format is in the vocabulary the codec table keys on (`docs/domain-model.md`), which is what decides the transports a viewer may open the stream over.

  The narrowing happens here rather than in a client: a listing a client filtered arrived carrying every group's streams.
  A group's listing leaves out the public streams for the same reason it leaves out another group's, that a member asked about their group.

  Rows carry no reader count and no rate.
  Those are the relay's operational state, answered by `Paths list` to a caller who can reach the relay's API.

  The group key is percent-encoded into `groupKeyQuery` before the request rather than read straight out of the environment.
  A group key is 43 characters of standard base64, so about half of all keys carry a `+` somewhere, and a `+` written into a query is a space by the time a server reads it: the answer is then "illegal base64 data", naming the position the `+` was at.
  The service is right to refuse it, the encoding being the caller's job, which is what `url.QueryEscape` does on the app's side (`backend/internal/groupclient`).
}
